<worksheet>
    <title>Party Expense &amp; National debt</title>

<objectives>
    <ul>
        <li>
                <p>Combining different models to create new models.</p>
        </li>
        
    </ul>
  </objectives>
  <section>
    <title>Party Expense</title>
    
    

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Suppose you are throwing a party and want  to share the expenses equally among all who attend.  The band      you hire costs $200 and everyone who comes will eat and drink about $3 worth of food and drink.
        </p>
    </introduction>
<question>
    <sidebyside widths="55% 35%" valign="middle">
        <p>
              What is  the  total  cost  for  2  people    to  attend?	5 people?	20 people?	In  these  cases  what  is  the charge  at  the  door,  assuming  it  is  split  equally?(Assume  that  you  and  the  band  members  don't pay.)
        </p>
        <table>
                <title>Flat fee band</title>
                
                
                <tabular top="major" bottom="major" left="minor" right="minor" row="minor" column="minor">
                    <row header="true">
                        <cell>Number of people attending</cell>
                        <cell>Total Cost </cell><cell>Cost per person</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>5</cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>10</cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>20</cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>30</cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>40</cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>x</cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>


                    
                </tabular>
            </table>
    </sidebyside>
</question>
<question>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Suppose the people who attend are only willing to pay at most $5 at the door.  State an equation you      must solve to answer this question. Solve it and answer the question: How many people can attend?
        </p>
    </introduction>
</question>
<question>
    <introduction>
 <sidebyside widths="55% 35%" valign="middle">
        <p>
            Now suppose the band gets greedy and ask that in addition to their base fee of $200 they get an additional amount equal to the square of the number of people who attend.  That is, if 30 people attend the band gets a total of <m>$200 + 30^2 = $1100</m>. If <m>x</m> people attend, what is the cost per person? (The cost of the food and drink is still the same.) 
        </p>

        <table>
                <title>Variable fee band</title>
                
                
                <tabular top="major" bottom="major" left="minor" right="minor" row="minor" column="minor">
                    <row header="true">
                        <cell>Number of people attending</cell>
                        <cell>Total Cost </cell><cell>Cost per person</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>5</cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>10</cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>20</cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>


                    <row>
                        <cell>x</cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>


                    
                </tabular>
            </table>
    </sidebyside>
</introduction>
</question>

<question>
    <introduction>
        <p>
           Consider the same situation as above, only this time the band is very famous and the people are willing to pay at most $50. How many can attend? State an equation you must solve to answer this   question.
        </p>
    </introduction>
</question>
  </section>
<section>
    <title>National Debt</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
          The population of the United States can be modeled by the function <md>P(t) = 0.226t^2+1.885t+204.72</md>, where <m>P(t)</m> is the population in millions and <m>t</m> is the number of years since 1970. The national debt can be modeled by the function <md>D(t) = 8215.1t^2-23035t+413525.6</md>, where <m>D(t)</m> is the national debt in millions of dollars and <m>t</m> is the number of years since 1970.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <question>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                According to these models, what was the national debt in 1982?
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </question>
    <question>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                According to these models, what was the population in 1982?
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </question>
    <question>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                According to these models, what was the average national debt per capita in 1982 (i.e debt per person)?
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </question>
    <question>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Find a new model, <m>A(t)</m>, for the average amount of national debt per capita (i.e. debt per person) in the United State t years since 1970
            </p>
        </introduction>
        
    </question>
    <question>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Use the model <m>A(t)</m> to estimate the average national debt per capita in 2011.
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </question>
    <question>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Determine when the national debt per capita is $10,000.  First write an equation you need to solve to  answer this question, solve it in Desmos,then write a sentence to summarize your answer.
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </question>
</section>
</worksheet>